1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,010
Three point six nine cents.

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:05,167
That is what it cost the United

3
00:00:05,167 --> 00:00:07,802
States Mint to make a single one-cent

4
00:00:07,802 --> 00:00:08,258
coin,

5
00:00:08,258 --> 00:00:11,254
in its twenty twenty-four fiscal year.

6
00:00:11,517 --> 00:00:14,208
So every new penny was a small

7
00:00:14,208 --> 00:00:15,077
loss.

8
00:00:15,187 --> 00:00:16,159
Add them up,

9
00:00:16,159 --> 00:00:17,903
and in that one year the Mint

10
00:00:17,903 --> 00:00:19,896
spent eighty-five point three million

11
00:00:19,896 --> 00:00:22,138
dollars more making pennies than the

12
00:00:22,138 --> 00:00:23,734
pennies were worth.

13
00:00:23,983 --> 00:00:26,099
So why did it take so long

14
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:27,253
to stop?

15
00:00:27,253 --> 00:00:29,852
And what happens to your change now?

16
00:00:30,117 --> 00:00:32,456
By the end of this video,

17
00:00:32,456 --> 00:00:34,528
you will know how a coin ends

18
00:00:34,528 --> 00:00:37,162
up costing more than it is worth,

19
00:00:37,162 --> 00:00:40,093
how stores round your change without it,

20
00:00:40,093 --> 00:00:43,061
and why the nickel may be next.

21
00:00:48,717 --> 00:00:52,057
Governments usually make money on coins.

22
00:00:52,057 --> 00:00:54,555
A quarter costs much less than twenty-five

23
00:00:54,555 --> 00:00:55,530
cents to make,

24
00:00:55,530 --> 00:00:58,028
so every quarter the Mint ships is

25
00:00:58,028 --> 00:00:59,627
a small profit.

26
00:00:59,667 --> 00:01:03,127
Economists call that profit seigniorage.

27
00:01:03,207 --> 00:01:04,865
In twenty twenty-four,

28
00:01:04,865 --> 00:01:07,509
quarters alone earned the Mint one hundred

29
00:01:07,509 --> 00:01:10,512
and sixty-five point six million dollars.

30
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:12,900
The penny went the other way.

31
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:15,756
Its cost jumped about twenty percent in

32
00:01:15,756 --> 00:01:16,504
one year,

33
00:01:16,504 --> 00:01:18,567
from about three cents to three point

34
00:01:18,567 --> 00:01:19,940
six nine cents.

35
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:22,022
According to the Treasury,

36
00:01:22,022 --> 00:01:23,773
it was only around one point three

37
00:01:23,773 --> 00:01:25,540
cents a decade earlier.

38
00:01:26,070 --> 00:01:27,975
The Mint shipped more than three billion

39
00:01:27,975 --> 00:01:30,070
new pennies in twenty twenty-four,

40
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:32,787
and every one of them lost money.

41
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:35,560
Here is another way to see it.

42
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:38,534
For every one dollar of pennies the

43
00:01:38,534 --> 00:01:39,479
Mint sent out,

44
00:01:39,479 --> 00:01:42,143
it spent about two dollars and sixty-nine

45
00:01:42,143 --> 00:01:44,680
cents more than that dollar was worth.

46
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:46,998
A dime costs under six cents to

47
00:01:46,998 --> 00:01:47,870
make.

48
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:48,828
A quarter,

49
00:01:48,828 --> 00:01:50,380
under fifteen.

50
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,650
Those coins pay their way.

51
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:54,923
The penny never could.

52
00:01:55,183 --> 00:01:58,083
And it was not a one-year accident.

53
00:01:58,083 --> 00:02:00,546
The Mint reports that both the penny

54
00:02:00,546 --> 00:02:02,735
and the nickel have cost more than

55
00:02:02,735 --> 00:02:05,472
their face value for nineteen fiscal years

56
00:02:05,472 --> 00:02:06,593
in a row.

57
00:02:06,593 --> 00:02:07,655
The nickel,

58
00:02:07,655 --> 00:02:08,716
worth five cents,

59
00:02:08,716 --> 00:02:11,166
cost thirteen point seven eight cents to

60
00:02:11,166 --> 00:02:12,039
make.

61
00:02:12,283 --> 00:02:14,449
Across all circulating coins,

62
00:02:14,449 --> 00:02:16,372
the Mint still made a profit,

63
00:02:16,372 --> 00:02:18,806
about ninety-nine point five million

64
00:02:18,806 --> 00:02:21,353
dollars in twenty twenty-four.

65
00:02:21,583 --> 00:02:23,501
But that was sixty percent less than

66
00:02:23,501 --> 00:02:24,953
the year before.

67
00:02:25,153 --> 00:02:27,405
The losing coins were eating into the

68
00:02:27,405 --> 00:02:28,456
winners.

69
00:02:28,717 --> 00:02:31,117
Let me draw the coin ledger.

70
00:02:31,137 --> 00:02:32,425
Here is the quarter,

71
00:02:32,425 --> 00:02:33,987
a big profit bar.

72
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:34,538
The dime,

73
00:02:34,538 --> 00:02:36,087
a smaller profit.

74
00:02:36,087 --> 00:02:37,502
Then below the line,

75
00:02:37,502 --> 00:02:38,437
the nickel,

76
00:02:38,437 --> 00:02:39,417
a loss.

77
00:02:39,417 --> 00:02:40,464
And the penny,

78
00:02:40,464 --> 00:02:42,347
the biggest loss of all.

79
00:02:42,457 --> 00:02:44,567
Two coins pay for themselves.

80
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:47,318
Two coins cost the government money every

81
00:02:47,318 --> 00:02:48,836
time they are made.

82
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:50,035
For years,

83
00:02:50,035 --> 00:02:53,263
that loss was simply accepted.

84
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:53,750
Then,

85
00:02:53,750 --> 00:02:55,263
in twenty twenty-five,

86
00:02:55,263 --> 00:02:58,276
the government finally pulled the plug.

87
00:03:08,917 --> 00:03:10,372
On November twelfth,

88
00:03:10,372 --> 00:03:11,827
twenty twenty-five,

89
00:03:11,827 --> 00:03:13,282
in Philadelphia,

90
00:03:13,282 --> 00:03:15,748
the Mint held a ceremonial strike of

91
00:03:15,748 --> 00:03:18,567
the final circulating penny.

92
00:03:18,937 --> 00:03:21,504
The Treasurer of the United States pressed

93
00:03:21,504 --> 00:03:22,697
the button.

94
00:03:22,907 --> 00:03:25,117
According to the St.

95
00:03:25,117 --> 00:03:25,739
Louis Fed,

96
00:03:25,739 --> 00:03:28,100
it ended two hundred and thirty-two years

97
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:29,987
of penny production.

98
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:31,314
In practice,

99
00:03:31,314 --> 00:03:34,370
the flow had stopped even earlier.

100
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:37,102
According to the Federal Reserve,

101
00:03:37,102 --> 00:03:39,516
it received its last new pennies from

102
00:03:39,516 --> 00:03:41,689
the Mint shortly after May twenty

103
00:03:41,689 --> 00:03:42,631
twenty-five,

104
00:03:42,631 --> 00:03:45,163
months before the ceremony.

105
00:03:45,417 --> 00:03:47,957
But the penny did not disappear.

106
00:03:48,137 --> 00:03:50,327
It is still legal tender.

107
00:03:50,417 --> 00:03:52,133
The Mint keeps making a few for

108
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:53,257
collectors.

109
00:03:53,257 --> 00:03:55,533
And the Federal Reserve keeps moving the

110
00:03:55,533 --> 00:03:57,167
old ones around.

111
00:03:57,267 --> 00:04:00,182
Its banks resumed taking penny deposits in

112
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:02,177
January twenty twenty-six,

113
00:04:02,177 --> 00:04:04,419
and today every one of its coin

114
00:04:04,419 --> 00:04:07,261
terminals fills penny orders again.

115
00:04:07,517 --> 00:04:09,937
How many pennies are out there?

116
00:04:10,147 --> 00:04:12,317
Nobody knows exactly.

117
00:04:12,467 --> 00:04:14,491
The Treasury talks about roughly one

118
00:04:14,491 --> 00:04:16,507
hundred and fourteen billion.

119
00:04:16,627 --> 00:04:18,445
The Mint has estimated three hundred

120
00:04:18,445 --> 00:04:20,317
billion in circulation.

121
00:04:20,437 --> 00:04:21,492
Either way,

122
00:04:21,492 --> 00:04:23,656
far more than the Mint says commerce

123
00:04:23,656 --> 00:04:24,527
needs.

124
00:04:24,737 --> 00:04:27,047
The Treasury says the Federal Reserve will

125
00:04:27,047 --> 00:04:29,358
keep recirculating the existing pennies

126
00:04:29,358 --> 00:04:31,305
for as long as possible.

127
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:34,840
Stopping production saves money.

128
00:04:34,930 --> 00:04:36,851
According to the Treasury,

129
00:04:36,851 --> 00:04:39,441
about fifty-six million dollars a year in

130
00:04:39,441 --> 00:04:41,120
materials alone.

131
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:43,942
That is small for the federal budget,

132
00:04:43,942 --> 00:04:45,632
but it ends a loss that grew

133
00:04:45,632 --> 00:04:48,288
every year the coin got more expensive

134
00:04:48,288 --> 00:04:49,371
to make.

135
00:04:49,617 --> 00:04:52,227
Let me draw the penny's journey.

136
00:04:52,397 --> 00:04:53,449
Here is the Mint,

137
00:04:53,449 --> 00:04:55,717
where new pennies used to start.

138
00:04:55,717 --> 00:04:56,683
Then the Fed,

139
00:04:56,683 --> 00:04:57,650
then the banks,

140
00:04:57,650 --> 00:04:58,864
then the stores,

141
00:04:58,864 --> 00:05:00,078
then your pocket,

142
00:05:00,078 --> 00:05:01,917
and often a jar.

143
00:05:02,017 --> 00:05:04,167
Now the first step is gone.

144
00:05:04,167 --> 00:05:05,953
The coins still circulate,

145
00:05:05,953 --> 00:05:08,024
but no new ones come in to

146
00:05:08,024 --> 00:05:10,954
replace those that vanish into jars.

147
00:05:11,217 --> 00:05:14,527
Which leaves a very practical question.

148
00:05:14,577 --> 00:05:16,895
What happens at the cash register when

149
00:05:16,895 --> 00:05:18,655
the pennies run out?

150
00:05:24,317 --> 00:05:26,696
The Treasury published guidance,

151
00:05:26,696 --> 00:05:28,017
not a law.

152
00:05:28,087 --> 00:05:29,804
It only applies to cash,

153
00:05:29,804 --> 00:05:31,955
and only to the final total,

154
00:05:31,955 --> 00:05:33,207
after tax.

155
00:05:33,367 --> 00:05:35,505
If the total ends in one,

156
00:05:35,505 --> 00:05:36,019
two,

157
00:05:36,019 --> 00:05:37,616
six or seven cents,

158
00:05:37,616 --> 00:05:38,757
round down.

159
00:05:38,807 --> 00:05:40,382
If it ends in three,

160
00:05:40,382 --> 00:05:40,889
four,

161
00:05:40,889 --> 00:05:41,930
eight or nine,

162
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:44,399
round up to the nearest five.

163
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:46,828
So a bill of four dollars and

164
00:05:46,828 --> 00:05:50,150
ninety-eight cents becomes five dollars.

165
00:05:50,190 --> 00:05:51,767
But a bill of four dollars and

166
00:05:51,767 --> 00:05:54,930
ninety-six cents becomes four ninety-five.

167
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:56,618
If you pay by card,

168
00:05:56,618 --> 00:05:58,670
nothing changes at all.

169
00:05:58,730 --> 00:06:01,260
You still pay to the exact cent.

170
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:03,004
And the rounding happens once,

171
00:06:03,004 --> 00:06:04,247
on the whole receipt,

172
00:06:04,247 --> 00:06:06,830
not on every item in your basket.

173
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:09,505
A cart of fifty items gets rounded

174
00:06:09,505 --> 00:06:10,252
one time,

175
00:06:10,252 --> 00:06:11,624
at the end.

176
00:06:11,883 --> 00:06:15,073
States are writing their own rules.

177
00:06:15,073 --> 00:06:17,701
Washington passed a law in twenty

178
00:06:17,701 --> 00:06:20,066
twenty-six that lets stores round cash

179
00:06:20,066 --> 00:06:21,616
payments the same way,

180
00:06:21,616 --> 00:06:23,793
but does not force them to.

181
00:06:24,053 --> 00:06:26,213
It took effect in June.

182
00:06:26,393 --> 00:06:29,265
Customers can still pay with exact change,

183
00:06:29,265 --> 00:06:32,763
and tax is added before any rounding.

184
00:06:32,983 --> 00:06:33,886
In Congress,

185
00:06:33,886 --> 00:06:35,971
the House and the Senate each passed

186
00:06:35,971 --> 00:06:38,264
their own version of a rounding bill,

187
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:40,093
called the Common Cents Act,

188
00:06:40,093 --> 00:06:42,083
in twenty twenty-six.

189
00:06:42,283 --> 00:06:43,560
As of August,

190
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:45,879
they had not agreed on one text,

191
00:06:45,879 --> 00:06:48,042
so it is not law yet.

192
00:06:48,283 --> 00:06:50,943
Does rounding cheat shoppers?

193
00:06:50,943 --> 00:06:53,253
The Richmond Fed studied it.

194
00:06:53,403 --> 00:06:55,552
Its researchers estimate the cost to

195
00:06:55,552 --> 00:06:58,131
consumers at about six million dollars a

196
00:06:58,131 --> 00:06:58,539
year,

197
00:06:58,539 --> 00:07:00,643
across the whole country.

198
00:07:00,893 --> 00:07:02,496
By our calculation,

199
00:07:02,496 --> 00:07:04,122
that is less than two cents per

200
00:07:04,122 --> 00:07:05,260
American,

201
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:06,325
per year.

202
00:07:06,583 --> 00:07:08,853
Let me draw the rounding wheel.

203
00:07:09,143 --> 00:07:10,789
Here are the last digits,

204
00:07:10,789 --> 00:07:12,343
zero to nine.

205
00:07:12,443 --> 00:07:14,943
One and two go down to zero.

206
00:07:14,943 --> 00:07:17,303
Three and four go up to five.

207
00:07:17,303 --> 00:07:20,103
Six and seven go down to five.

208
00:07:20,103 --> 00:07:22,047
Eight and nine go up to the

209
00:07:22,047 --> 00:07:23,203
next ten.

210
00:07:23,403 --> 00:07:24,881
Half the time you win a cent

211
00:07:24,881 --> 00:07:25,783
or two.

212
00:07:25,783 --> 00:07:27,820
Half the time you lose one.

213
00:07:28,083 --> 00:07:30,333
This is not the first time America

214
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:32,733
has had to rethink the penny.

215
00:07:32,763 --> 00:07:34,631
And it is not the first country

216
00:07:34,631 --> 00:07:35,932
to drop one.

217
00:07:41,583 --> 00:07:43,368
In nineteen eighty-two,

218
00:07:43,368 --> 00:07:45,401
the Mint changed the recipe to save

219
00:07:45,401 --> 00:07:46,453
money.

220
00:07:46,653 --> 00:07:48,892
The penny went from ninety-five percent

221
00:07:48,892 --> 00:07:50,928
copper to almost ninety-eight percent

222
00:07:50,928 --> 00:07:51,315
zinc,

223
00:07:51,315 --> 00:07:53,543
with only a little copper.

224
00:07:53,773 --> 00:07:54,638
At the time,

225
00:07:54,638 --> 00:07:56,634
the change was expected to save the

226
00:07:56,634 --> 00:07:58,852
Treasury around twenty-five million

227
00:07:58,852 --> 00:08:00,339
dollars a year.

228
00:08:00,583 --> 00:08:02,633
Canada went further.

229
00:08:02,783 --> 00:08:04,213
In May twenty twelve,

230
00:08:04,213 --> 00:08:06,636
its finance minister struck the country's

231
00:08:06,636 --> 00:08:08,913
last penny in Winnipeg.

232
00:08:08,913 --> 00:08:11,546
Its mint stopped sending pennies to banks

233
00:08:11,546 --> 00:08:14,253
in February twenty thirteen.

234
00:08:14,423 --> 00:08:16,535
Each one cost about one point six

235
00:08:16,535 --> 00:08:18,984
cents Canadian cents to make.

236
00:08:19,250 --> 00:08:21,736
The government expected to save eleven

237
00:08:21,736 --> 00:08:24,200
million Canadian dollars a year.

238
00:08:24,430 --> 00:08:26,796
At the exchange rate of January twenty

239
00:08:26,796 --> 00:08:27,419
thirteen,

240
00:08:27,419 --> 00:08:30,600
that is about eleven million US dollars.

241
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:33,241
Canadians have rounded their cash payments

242
00:08:33,241 --> 00:08:34,568
ever since.

243
00:08:34,817 --> 00:08:37,107
Let me draw the timeline.

244
00:08:37,107 --> 00:08:38,527
Nineteen eighty-two,

245
00:08:38,527 --> 00:08:41,567
the copper penny becomes a zinc penny.

246
00:08:41,567 --> 00:08:42,674
Twenty thirteen,

247
00:08:42,674 --> 00:08:45,237
Canada stops handing out pennies.

248
00:08:45,237 --> 00:08:46,407
Twenty twenty-five,

249
00:08:46,407 --> 00:08:49,187
the United States strikes its last one.

250
00:08:49,187 --> 00:08:49,935
Each time,

251
00:08:49,935 --> 00:08:51,507
the same reason.

252
00:08:51,507 --> 00:08:54,037
The metal cost more than the coin.

253
00:08:54,283 --> 00:08:57,241
And that same reason is now staring

254
00:08:57,241 --> 00:08:59,484
at the next coin up.

255
00:09:05,150 --> 00:09:07,696
The nickel is a bigger loss per

256
00:09:07,696 --> 00:09:10,560
coin than the penny ever was.

257
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:13,615
In twenty twenty-four it cost almost

258
00:09:13,615 --> 00:09:17,150
fourteen cents to make a five-cent coin.

259
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:20,339
The Mint lost about seventeen point seven

260
00:09:20,339 --> 00:09:22,965
million dollars on nickels that year,

261
00:09:22,965 --> 00:09:25,688
less in total than on pennies.

262
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:29,210
But dropping the nickel would be harder.

263
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:31,549
Without pennies and nickels,

264
00:09:31,549 --> 00:09:33,745
cash would round to the nearest ten

265
00:09:33,745 --> 00:09:34,620
cents.

266
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:37,674
The Richmond Fed estimates that would cost

267
00:09:37,674 --> 00:09:40,679
consumers about about fifty-six million

268
00:09:40,679 --> 00:09:41,905
dollars a year,

269
00:09:41,905 --> 00:09:44,409
nearly ten times the cost of rounding

270
00:09:44,409 --> 00:09:45,760
to five cents.

271
00:09:46,017 --> 00:09:48,486
There is also a bigger trend behind

272
00:09:48,486 --> 00:09:49,827
all of this.

273
00:09:49,847 --> 00:09:51,867
According to the St.

274
00:09:51,867 --> 00:09:52,420
Louis Fed,

275
00:09:52,420 --> 00:09:54,519
cash was used in only about fourteen

276
00:09:54,519 --> 00:09:57,217
percent of payments in twenty twenty-four.

277
00:09:57,617 --> 00:09:58,745
Every year,

278
00:09:58,745 --> 00:10:01,417
fewer people need small coins at all.

279
00:10:01,457 --> 00:10:03,510
That makes it easier for governments to

280
00:10:03,510 --> 00:10:04,857
stop making them.

281
00:10:05,027 --> 00:10:05,952
For the Mint,

282
00:10:05,952 --> 00:10:08,085
ending the penny means less zinc and

283
00:10:08,085 --> 00:10:09,247
copper to buy,

284
00:10:09,247 --> 00:10:10,645
fewer coins to strike,

285
00:10:10,645 --> 00:10:12,305
and one less loss on its books

286
00:10:12,305 --> 00:10:14,327
every single year.

287
00:10:14,617 --> 00:10:15,348
For shoppers,

288
00:10:15,348 --> 00:10:17,223
it mostly means a slightly different

289
00:10:17,223 --> 00:10:19,322
number at the cash register.

290
00:10:19,583 --> 00:10:21,833
Let me draw the trade-off.

291
00:10:21,833 --> 00:10:22,823
On one side,

292
00:10:22,823 --> 00:10:24,855
the cost of making coins that lose

293
00:10:24,855 --> 00:10:25,873
money.

294
00:10:25,873 --> 00:10:27,108
On the other side,

295
00:10:27,108 --> 00:10:29,313
the cost of rounding without them.

296
00:10:29,313 --> 00:10:30,389
For the penny,

297
00:10:30,389 --> 00:10:32,365
the minting loss was far bigger than

298
00:10:32,365 --> 00:10:33,843
the rounding cost.

299
00:10:33,853 --> 00:10:34,900
For the nickel,

300
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:36,375
by these estimates,

301
00:10:36,375 --> 00:10:38,299
rounding would cost more than the minting

302
00:10:38,299 --> 00:10:39,053
loss.

303
00:10:39,053 --> 00:10:40,956
That is one reason the nickel is

304
00:10:40,956 --> 00:10:42,402
a harder call.

305
00:10:42,650 --> 00:10:45,155
So why did America stop making the

306
00:10:45,155 --> 00:10:46,210
penny?

307
00:10:46,210 --> 00:10:48,200
Because a coin is not just money.

308
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:49,260
It is a product,

309
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:50,141
made of metal,

310
00:10:50,141 --> 00:10:51,230
with a cost.

311
00:10:51,230 --> 00:10:53,065
When the cost of the product rises

312
00:10:53,065 --> 00:10:54,879
above the value printed on it,

313
00:10:54,879 --> 00:10:57,110
every new coin is a loss.

314
00:10:57,350 --> 00:11:00,150
The penny crossed that line years ago.

315
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:02,270
The only surprise is how long it

316
00:11:02,270 --> 00:11:03,577
took to stop.

317
00:11:03,817 --> 00:11:05,877
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

318
00:11:05,877 --> 00:11:08,335
we follow the money behind another hidden

319
00:11:08,335 --> 00:11:10,447
link of the world economy.

320
00:11:10,547 --> 00:11:12,737
This is The Hidden Economics.
